        <h3>1970 to 1973: Motown and Funk Lead the Way</h3>
        <p>The early years are the thinnest on the list.</p>
        <p>"I Want You Back" by The Jackson 5 reached number one on January 31, 1970, and Billy Preston's "Will It Go Round in Circles" took two weeks in July 1973.</p>
        <p>James Brown's "Get on the Good Foot" is here too, at number 99 on the 1972 year-end list. The <a href="/blog/songs/james-brown-get-on-the-good-foot/">Get on the Good Foot</a> page tells the rest.</p>

        <h3>1974 to 1976: The Floor Gets Louder</h3>
        <p>{{TOP_ERA}} holds the most songs, with {{TOP_ERA_N}}.</p>
        <p>It runs from "Love's Theme" in February 1974 to "Disco Duck" in October 1976.</p>
        <p>On the weekly chart, Elton John and Kiki Dee's "Don't Go Breaking My Heart" gave way to "You Should Be Dancing" on September 4, 1976.</p>
        <p>"(Shake, Shake, Shake) Shake Your Booty" took over a week later, and "Play That Funky Music" followed.</p>

        <h3>1977 and 1978: Saturday Night Fever Takes Over</h3>
        <p>"Dancing Queen" reached number one on April 9, 1977.</p>
        <p>"Don't Give Up on Us" held the top spot in between, and Thelma Houston's "Don't Leave Me This Way" followed on April 23.</p>
        <p>The Bee Gees' "Night Fever" and Andy Gibb's "Shadow Dancing" then ran for 15 weeks at number one between them in 1978.</p>

        <h3>1979: The Floor at Full Volume</h3>
        <p>1979 gives the list its busiest single year, with six number ones: "Da Ya Think I'm Sexy?," "Hot Stuff," "Ring My Bell," "Bad Girls," "Good Times" and "Don't Stop 'til You Get Enough."</p>
        <p>For the genre's full arc, read <a href="/blog/genres/disco-music-of-the-70s/">70s disco music</a>, which follows how it peaked and nearly died.</p>

        <h2 id="streaks">The Longest Dance Floor Streaks</h2>
        <p>Staying power at the top is the clearest sign a dance song landed.</p>
        <p>These {{N_LONG}} songs held number one for two weeks or more.</p>

