        <h3>"Night Fever" and "Shadow Dancing" Own 1978</h3>
        <p>The Bee Gees' "Night Fever" reached number one on March 18, 1978, and stayed for eight weeks.</p>
        <p>Andy Gibb's "Shadow Dancing" reached the top on June 17, 1978, and held for seven.</p>
        <p>Read the full <a href="/blog/songs/night-fever/">Night Fever</a> story, then spin the <a href="/pages/random-70s-song">Random 70s Song Generator</a> for a surprise.</p>

        <h3>Donna Summer's Summer of 1979</h3>
        <p>"Hot Stuff" spent three weeks at number one in June 1979, and "Ring My Bell" took over on June 30.</p>
        <p>"Bad Girls" then held the top for five weeks from July 14, and Chic's "Good Times" followed on August 18.</p>
        <p>The full <a href="/blog/songs/bad-girls/">Bad Girls</a> story has the rest.</p>

        <h2 id="missed">Dance Songs That Never Hit Number One</h2>
        <p>{{NOT_N1}} songs on the list never topped the weekly Hot 100.</p>
        <p>Each is here because the year-end list proves it was played everywhere.</p>
        <ul>
{{NOT_N1_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>A Rank Is Not a Peak</h3>
        <p>The year-end list measures a whole year of chart life, not the highest weekly position.</p>
        <p>So these ranks say how big the song was, not whether it climbed to the top.</p>
        <p>For the decade's biggest hits, see the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-number-one-hits/">70s number one hits</a> page, and for the quieter side of the same chart, the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-love-songs/">70s love songs</a> list.</p>
        <p>The decade's full story starts at <a href="/">Music of the 70s</a>.</p>

